 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How cold is too cold to walk a dog?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Go by the feels-like temperature. By the POOPIEMATE walk safety index, small dogs need care below a feels-like 10°C (50°F), and around 0°C (32°F) even an average dog should take a short potty-first walk. A double-coated dog used to the cold can walk as usual at -5°C (23°F)."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can I walk my dog below freezing?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "A healthy double-coated adult is often fine down to about -5°C (23°F). Small, short-coated or senior dogs should wear a coat and keep it to a 5–10 minute potty walk. Below a feels-like -10°C (14°F) the base level is 5, so even a cold-hardy double-coated dog should keep it short."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Should small dogs skip walks in winter?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Rather than skipping, go out briefly and more often, in daylight, with a coat. Walks matter for potty and stress relief, so not going out at all can cause other problems."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is it OK to walk my dog in the snow?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Snow soaks the fur and drains body heat faster, so count it one level higher. Dry your dog off right after, and always wash the paws if the path was salted."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How long should winter walks be?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "At levels 1–2 walk as usual, at level 3 keep it to 15–20 minutes, at level 4 go short and potty first, and at level 5 potty only within five minutes. Make up for the rest with sniffing games or tug indoors."
   }
  }
 ]
